# Line-ending normalization. This repo is developed on Windows but builds/runs on Linux
# (CI runners, Docker images). Without this, a CRLF<->LF flip silently changes a file's bytes.
#
# For Flyway migrations this is not cosmetic: Flyway records a CHECKSUM per applied migration and
# fails the app at boot if a previously-applied file's checksum changes. A line-ending normalization
# of an already-shipped *.sql migration would therefore break startup on staging/prod while every
# fresh dev/CI database stays green. Pin migrations (and all SQL) to LF so the checksum is stable
# regardless of the author's OS or git autocrlf setting.

# Let git decide for unmarked files (normalizes to LF in the repo, checks out native on Windows).
* text=auto

# Windows batch launchers in the client distribution bundle. A .cmd with bare LF endings
# misbehaves around labels and goto, so these must be CRLF in the working tree on every
# platform — including a Linux checkout, from which a release may be built.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# The client's .env template must be LF. Docker passes each line of an env file into the
# container verbatim, so a CRLF checkout gives every setting an invisible trailing carriage
# return: passwords that are silently wrong, JDBC URLs that will not parse. Without this pin
# it inherits `text=auto` and checks out CRLF on Windows, and a release built from that clone
# ships the fault to the client.
*.example   text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf

# Force LF in the working tree too for these — never CRLF, on any platform.
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Binary assets — never normalize.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.jar   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
